<sn><md>### City Waste

**City waste**, also called **municipal solid waste (MSW)**, is the waste generated from homes, markets, offices, schools, restaurants, streets, and other urban activities.

Types of City Waste

| Type                          | Examples                              |
| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| **Organic waste**             | Food waste, vegetable waste, leaves   |
| **Paper waste**               | Newspapers, cardboard, paper          |
| **Plastic waste**             | Bottles, bags, packaging              |
| **Glass waste**               | Bottles, containers                   |
| **Metal waste**               | Cans, tins, scrap metal               |
| **Electronic waste**          | Computers, phones, electronic devices |
| **Hazardous household waste** | Batteries, paints, chemicals          |

Effects of City Waste

Improper management can cause:

* **Air pollution** from open burning
* **Water pollution** from leachate
* Soil contamination
* Bad odor
* Breeding of **flies, mosquitoes, and rats**
* Spread of diseases
* Blockage of drainage systems
* Release of **greenhouse gases**, particularly methane, from decomposing organic waste

```text id="y1y3we"
City activities
      ↓
Municipal waste
      ↓
Improper disposal
      ↓
 ┌────┼────┐
 ↓    ↓    ↓
Soil Water Air
 ↓    ↓    ↓
 └────┼────┘
      ↓
Health + Environmental problems
```

</md></sn><sn><md>### Hospital Waste vs City Waste

| Feature           | Hospital Waste                                       | City Waste                                                      |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Main source       | Healthcare facilities                                | Urban activities                                                |
| Main concern      | Infection and hazardous medical materials            | Large volume and environmental pollution                        |
| Examples          | Needles, blood-contaminated materials, medicines     | Food, paper, plastic, glass                                     |
| Major risks       | Infection, sharps injuries, chemical exposure        | Disease vectors, pollution, odor, methane                       |
| Special treatment | Often requires segregation and specialized treatment | Usually involves collection, recycling, composting and disposal |

Key difference

**Hospital waste** is particularly concerning because a portion can be **infectious or hazardous**.

**City waste** is mainly a **large-volume municipal waste problem**, containing both biodegradable and non-biodegradable materials.

Summary

* **Hospital waste** comes from healthcare facilities.
* It includes **infectious waste, sharps, pathological waste, pharmaceuticals, chemicals, and radioactive materials**.
* Improper hospital-waste disposal can cause **infection, injuries, and environmental contamination**.
* **City waste** is also called **municipal solid waste (MSW)**.
* It includes **food, paper, plastic, glass, metals, and other urban wastes**.
* Improper city-waste disposal causes **air, water, and soil pollution**, bad odors, disease vectors, and greenhouse-gas emissions.
* Both require proper **segregation, collection, treatment, recycling/recovery, and safe disposal**.
</md></sn>